    class="products-hero"
    style="background: linear-gradient(rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.28), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.18)), url('/assets/img/cleaning-machine/web-cleaning-line.jpg') center/cover no-repeat; background-attachment: fixed;"
  >
    <div class="container products-hero-inner">
      <span class="products-hero-kicker" data-aos="fade-down" data-aos-duration="600">Second Product Line</span>
      <h1 data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-duration="700" data-aos-delay="100">Web Cleaning Systems &amp; Contact Cleaners</h1>
      <p data-aos="fade-up" data-aos-duration="700" data-aos-delay="200">
        Professional industrial cleaning solutions for PCB, FPC, polarizer,
        backlight, optical film, SMT, LCM, and other precision manufacturing applications.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="category-info-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:900px;">
      <h2>Two ways to clean a surface, and everything else is built to your sample</h2>
      <p>
        A contact cleaner runs an elastomer roller against the surface. The roller lifts loose
        particles off, then transfers them onto an adhesive paper roll that winds them away
        permanently. It removes what an air knife or a brush leaves behind, down to particles
        too small to see.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every machine we build is <strong>made to the sample you send us</strong>. There is no
        catalogue model that fits your line — the belt, the roller, the gap and the static
        package are all chosen from your material. What follows is how those four choices are
        actually made.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section cm-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1080px;">
      <h2 class="cm-h2">1 — Contact or non-contact</h2>
      <p class="cm-lede">The first question is whether the working surface can be touched at all.</p>
      <div class="cm-two">
        <div class="cm-card">
          <span class="cm-tag">Contact cleaning</span>
          <h3>The roller touches the surface</h3>
          <p>
            An elastomer roller runs against the material and lifts particles off it. This is the
            more thorough of the two — it takes off what is stuck, not just what is loose — and it
            is what most PCB, FPC, CCL and sheet applications use.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Double-sided, double-pass: 4 cleaning rollers plus 2 collection rollers</li>
            <li>Particle removal to 99.9 %</li>
            <li>Roller gap adjustable 0.1–6.0 mm, one-touch, with a digital readout</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="cm-card">
          <span class="cm-tag">Non-contact transport</span>
          <h3>Nothing touches the effective area</h3>
          <p>
            Magnetic levitation drive moves the material without mechanical contact on the
            working face. Used where the surface cannot take roller pressure at all — ultra-thin
            film, coated optical layers, or material that marks under any load.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Frictionless transport, no drive marks on the surface</li>
            <li>Handles ultra-thin and warped material without curling or creasing the corners</li>
            <li>Cleaning still happens; the contact is managed rather than eliminated</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section cm-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1080px;">
      <h2 class="cm-h2">2 — The belt</h2>
      <p class="cm-lede">
        The belt is chosen from your sample, and thickness is what usually decides it. Send us
        the material and we will tell you which one your line needs.
      </p>
      <div class="cm-three">
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Flat belt</h3>
          <p>The general-purpose transport surface. Full contact underneath, even support across the whole sheet — the default where the back face is not critical.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Ribbed belt</h3>
          <p>Narrow strips instead of a continuous surface, so the material rests on lines rather than a full face. Less contact area underneath, which matters when the back side has to stay unmarked.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Slat conveyor</h3>
          <p>A slatted transport structure rather than a belt. Used for rigid boards and for light guide plates, where a belt would flex the material or leave an impression.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cm-note">
        Effective cleaning width runs to 1500 mm and length is unrestricted; material thickness
        from 0.022 mm up to 6 mm depending on configuration. The belt and the gap are set
        together — that pairing is what your sample determines.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section cm-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1080px;">
      <h2 class="cm-h2">3 — Rollers and consumables</h2>
      <p class="cm-lede">
        Two consumables do the actual work: the elastomer roller that lifts particles off your
        material, and the adhesive paper roll that takes them off the roller.
      </p>
      <div class="cm-two">
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Elastomer cleaning roller</h3>
          <p>
            Korean-imported RTV liquid silicone. <strong>Rollers are graded by tack</strong> — that
            grade is the single most important choice on the machine.
          </p>
          <p class="cm-small">
            Too little tack and fine particles stay on your material. Too much and the roller
            pulls at the surface, which on thin film or a soft coating causes its own defects.
            Send a sample and we match the grade to it rather than guessing from a datasheet.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Adhesive paper roll</h3>
          <p>
            Paper, two rolls per machine — one upper, one lower. <strong>3.0 inch core, 20 m per
            roll, 800 D tack, knife-free changeover.</strong>
          </p>
          <p class="cm-small">
            The roll is what makes removal permanent: particles go from your surface to the
            roller to the paper, and wind away. Rollers and rolls pull out on a drawer mechanism,
            so a change does not mean dismantling the machine.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="page-section cm-block">
    <div class="container" style="max-width:1080px;">
      <h2 class="cm-h2">4 — The static package</h2>
      <p class="cm-lede">
        Cleaning a charged surface does not hold. The charge pulls particles back out of the air
        within seconds of the material leaving the machine, so a cleaner without static control
        is solving half the problem.
      </p>
      <div class="cm-two">
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Standard: two ST-G bars</h3>
          <p>
            The usual build is <strong>two <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-g-series">ST-G ionizing bars</a></strong>,
            neutralizing the material as it passes. Ion balance ±50 V, 6.5 kV DC pulse, DC 24 V —
            the bars are made to the width of your machine.
          </p>
          <p class="cm-small">
            The <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-e-series">ST-E</a> is specified instead on
            wide machines where balance has to hold across the full span, and the
            <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-f-series">ST-F</a> where line speed is high
            enough that a standard bar cannot neutralize in time.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="cm-card">
          <h3>Optional: ionizing blowers</h3>
          <p>
            Some machines are built with <a href="/products/static-eliminator/st-104a">ST104A blowers</a>
            mounted low on both sides in addition to the bars.
          </p>
          <p class="cm-small">
            A bar neutralizes along a line; a blower moves ionized air across an area, including
            the underside and the exit zone. Tell us if your material charges again after it
            leaves the machine and we will quote the arrangement that covers it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cm-cta">
        <p>
          <strong>What to send for a quote</strong>
          A sample of your material, its thickness, the working width, your line speed, and the
          conveyor height you need to match. That is enough for us to specify the belt, the
          roller grade, the gap and the static package — and to quote a machine rather than a
          model number.
        </p>
        <a href="/contact" class="btn btn-warning text-uppercase px-4 py-2">Send Your Sample</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

